# THE CREW CONSOLE - what a console BECOMES when it boards something.
#
# The missing half of beaming down. `boarding_go_down` changes what the console IS -
# CONSOLE_TYPE, the roles, the crew seat, and which ship the client is assigned to, so
# the interior view would draw the boarded station - but it does not send the GUI task
# anywhere. Nothing in the library can: a reroute needs a LABEL, and labels are MAST.
#
# So the console stayed on whatever screen pressed the button, which is the ePADD
# Boarding Party app. Reported exactly that way: "there is no crew console, it does show
# I am on the outpost and I can beam up". The morph had worked; nobody had opened the map.
#
# This is the addon's job rather than the mission's, so every boarding mission gets a
# console without writing one. A mission that wants its own simply routes
# `boarding_went_down` itself - the signal carries the client and the host.


# One console, one character, one interior.
#
# `gui_boarding_console()` is the library's builder: the map in its own section, and
# beside it who you are, where you are standing, and what you can do there.
=== boarding_crew_console
    gui_boarding_console()
    # A FUNCTION, not a jump back to this label. Repainting re-sends every widget on the
    # screen to this console over the network, and a watcher would do it forever.
    on change boarding_console_revision():
        gui_boarding_console_tick()
    await gui()
    ->END


# Going down opens the map. `//shared/signal` because the signal is emitted once per
# console that boards, and `gui_reroute_client` takes that one client - running this
# per-console as well would reroute everybody every time anybody boarded.
#
# ONLY when there is somewhere to walk. A dialogue-only party (no `site=` on the
# invitation) has no interior, and sending it to a map of nothing would replace a screen
# that works with an empty one.
//shared/signal/boarding_went_down
    ->END if BOARDING_HOST is None or BOARDING_HOST == 0
    gui_reroute_client(BOARDING_CLIENT, boarding_crew_console)
    ->END


# And coming back takes the map away again. `boarding_go_up` has already restored
# CONSOLE_TYPE and put the client back on its own ship; this is the screen half. Without
# it a returning console would sit on the crew console still, drawing the interior of a
# station it has left.
//shared/signal/boarding_came_back
    ->END if BOARDING_CONSOLE is None
    gui_reroute_client(BOARDING_CLIENT, boarding_crew_home)
    ->END


# The way home, run ON the returning console so `client_id` is that console.
#
# NOT EVERYONE GOES HOME THE SAME WAY, and this is the legacy console's recipe rather
# than a new one, because it was paid for: `common_console_show` opens with
# `jump select_console_clear_ready if _ship_id == 0 or not has_role(..., "__player__")`,
# and the main screen is usually client 0 - the SERVER console, which has no ship. That
# drops it into the CLIENT console picker, whose `select_console` reads
# `console_select_item`, a task variable set in `client_main` that the server's GUI task
# never ran. A NameError and a dead screen.
=== boarding_crew_home
    # `bcc_` PREFIXED, and that is not style. `boarding_home_ship` is a LIBRARY FUNCTION
    # (procedural/gui/boarding_gui.py, re-exported through gui/__init__.py), so it is a
    # MAST global - and assigning to one is `Variable assignment to a keyword`, a COMPILE
    # error that takes the whole story to 0 labels. I named a local after the accessor I
    # had just added, which is the easiest version of this mistake to make.
    bcc_back = get_inventory_value(client_id, "CONSOLE_TYPE", "helm")
    jump show_main_game_screen if bcc_back == "mainscreen"
    bcc_ship = sbs.get_ship_of_client(client_id)
    jump show_main_game_screen if bcc_ship == 0 or not has_role(bcc_ship, "__player__")
    jump common_console_show
